<Layout title="Why Vankard exists"><section class="page-hero grid-paper"><span class="eyebrow">Why we exist</span><h1>The funnel wasn’t broken<br/>at the top.<br/><em>It was broken at the door.</em></h1><p>Vankard starts where most acquisition plans stop:<br/>the moment a visitor arrives.</p></section><section class="section split"><div class="founder-panel"><span class="eyebrow">The founding belief</span><span class="giant-quote">“</span><h3>Conversion first.<br/>Retention next.<br/><em>Then acquisition.</em></h3><span class="eyebrow">Zak / Founder, Vankard</span></div><div class="section-copy"><span class="eyebrow">From the founder</span><h2>Fix the door<br/><em>before the next ad.</em></h2><p>I have spent 14 years in marketing. The pattern kept repeating: businesses invested in getting people to their website, while the experience after arrival received far less attention.</p><p>A visitor with a real question could arrive ready to talk and find silence. By the time someone followed up, the moment had passed.</p><p>I built Vankard around a simple order: recover the conversations you are missing, optimize the experience, then scale acquisition. Vanka is the sales layer I wanted businesses to have before spending more on traffic.</p></div></section><section class="section three-grid principles">{[['01','Context before scripts','Understand the offer and the buyer before deciding what to say.'],['02','A useful next step','A conversation should help people decide, including when the answer is “not yet.”'],['03','Progress before scale','Improve the system you have before asking it to handle more demand.']].map(([n,t,d])=><article><span class="eyebrow">{n}</span><h3>{t}</h3><p>{d}</p></article>)}</section><CTA/></Layout>
